frame rates above 60 fps ultra wide  resolutions and better ray trace  reflections these are some of the unique  benefits gamers will get when playing  marvel spider-man's pc release on their  powerful gaming rigs  but as cool as all these features are i  already personally experienced a great  looking and running version of  spider-man on my ps5 what i haven't had  the chance to do is play it on the go  until now so screw all those fancy  features and say hello to spider-man  running on the steam deck  [Music]  but is it a good experience the answer  is mostly a big old thumbs up but with a  few caveats before we start i want to  address that everything we talk about  here is based on our time with a  pre-release version of the game sony  told us up front that they are still  working on optimization and warned us  about certain issues they plan to fix in  fact we got a big fat update just a few  days before this video launched offering  some major improvements so it's possible  some of the issues we talk about will be  improved down the line in future patches  let's get right into the good news which  is that spider-man on steam deck is a  really solid experience the majority of  the game swinging around the city  beating up bad guys doing side  activities all comfortably hits frames  above 30 fps sadly we couldn't get the  game to hit 60fps even at the lowest  settings and using dynamic resolution as  well as upscale methods like amd's fsr  2.0 set to ultra performance although  considering what it does to the image  even if it did hit 60fps you probably  wouldn't want to use it anyways but  we'll touch more on fsr 2.2 in a moment  so forget 60fps and instead look to  target 30. the thing is like spiderman  himself the frame rate likes to swing  around and fluctuate wildly which  creates a pretty unpleasant experience  while playing the simple solution is to  lock the game to 30 fps normally to do  this you'll need to set the resolution  to exclusive full screen and then you  get the refresh rate option but on steam  deck it is not accessible thankfully  there is a simple fix using steam deck's  built-in fps limiter open up the quick  access menu go to performance set the  frame rate limit to 30 and you're all  set smooth 30fps web swinging in the  palm of your hand sure you might miss  the 60fps especially if you're coming  from the ps5 or playing on a desktop but  considering that the original ps4  version of spider-man was locked to 30  fps and therefore designed around that  frame rate playing on the steam deck  doesn't feel like a compromised  experience at least when you're  spider-man there are a few sections in  the game where you play as peter parker  or mary jane in indoor environments with  higher detail and here the frame rate is  less stable fluctuating from 30 fps down  to 25 just enough to be noticeable and  annoying but because the gameplay in  these parts is mostly just walking  around it's not a deal breaker same goes  for the cutscenes which usually hit 30  but can dip in spots this is where we  return to our upscale methods turning on  fsr 2.0 to quality mode does fix this  right up locking everything to 30fps  but it does come with ghosting and other  visual oddities you'll have to  compromise with if you choose to turn it  on  sony did tell us the current build  offers preliminary support for fsr 2.0  and further development is ongoing for  image quality improvements so it's  possible fsr 2.0 might be more effective  in the future and look better the other  upscale option is insomniac games  temporal injection called igti which  switches between multiple upscale  algorithms including fsr 2.0 personally  i didn't notice much of a difference in  image and performance between the two on  the steam deck so take your pick but  what about the rest of the graphic  settings the sweet spot seems to be  medium across the board it creates a  pretty good looking image on the steam  deck's 800p screen dropping down to low  didn't really give any significant boost  to performance to be worth the loss in  visuals and going higher would cause the  game to crash  once you get everything all set up the  game looks pretty nice on the steam deck  cut scenes appropriately scale to the  16x10 screen filling up everything  nicely although menus do not but it's  less noticeable speaking of menus they  mostly play pretty well on the tiny  handheld screen although i do find that  text can be a little all over the place  in spots sometimes it's nice and large  and easily readable and other times it  can be pretty tiny but i'll say that  compared to some other games where i  struggled with the ui being scaled down  to such a tiny size i didn't feel that  way with spider-man and found it to be a  pretty natural fit on the handheld so  should you play spider-man on your steam  deck  abso-freaking-lutely small performance  issues aside the game feels like a  perfect fit for the portable pc it plays  great and the nature of the open world  makes it really easy to just turn on and  do a few quick side activities during  lunch in fact i made the effort to spend  most of my time with the pc version of  spider-man on the steam deck and despite  having already 100 of the game twice and  thinking i didn't really need to touch  it again i found myself having a great  time playing it on the handheld
 

